CONCEPT 2: TEMPERATURE, TOP-P, TOP-K
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

DEFINITION
----------
Three knobs that control how an LLM picks its next token. They don't change what
the model knows — they change how confidently or randomly it acts on what it knows.

ANALOGY
-------
You're in an exam, narrowed down to three options: A (70% sure), B (20%), C (10%).

Temperature = 0   → forced to always pick most confident answer. Always A. Robotic.
Temperature = 1   → spin a weighted wheel. A: 70%, B: 20%, C: 10%. Natural.
Temperature = 2   → drunk during exam. Wheel nearly flat. Wrong answers come up often.

Top-p = 0.9       → only consider answers that together account for 90% confidence.
                    If you're 95% sure about A alone → just pick A.
                    If split 40/35/25 → all three are in play. Adapts to certainty.

Top-k = 2         → only consider top 2 options regardless of probability distribution.
                    Blunt. Inflexible. Less used than top-p in production.

THE MECHANICS — WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS
---------------------------------------
Model produces logits (raw scores) for every token in vocabulary:
  raw_logits = model.forward(input_tokens)  # shape: [vocab_size]

Temperature divides logits before softmax:
  probs = softmax(logits / temperature)

  Low temp (0.1):  logit differences AMPLIFIED → top token dominates
  Temp = 1:        no change, default distribution
  High temp (2.0): logit differences COMPRESSED → everything more equal

import torch
import torch.nn.functional as F

logits = torch.tensor([2.0, 1.0, 0.5, 0.1, -1.0])

# T=0.1 → almost deterministic
softmax(logits / 0.1) → [0.9987, 0.0012, 0.0001, 0.0000, 0.0000]

# T=1.0 → natural distribution
softmax(logits / 1.0) → [0.5765, 0.2120, 0.1285, 0.0430, 0.0159]

# T=2.0 → flat, random
softmax(logits / 2.0) → [0.3955, 0.2393, 0.1807, 0.1228, 0.0617]

TOP-P (NUCLEUS SAMPLING)
------------------------
Sort tokens by probability (highest first).
Take tokens from the top until cumulative sum exceeds p.
Sample ONLY from those tokens (the "nucleus").

probs = [0.50, 0.30, 0.10, 0.07, 0.03]  # top-p = 0.9

Token 0: cumsum 0.50 → keep
Token 1: cumsum 0.80 → keep
Token 2: cumsum 0.90 → keep (boundary)
Token 3: cumsum 0.97 → EXCLUDE
Token 4: cumsum 1.00 → EXCLUDE

Nucleus = tokens 0, 1, 2. Sample from these only.

WHY TOP-P BEATS TOP-K FOR AGENTS
----------------------------------
Top-k = 50 always samples from 50 tokens.
  Even if model is 99% sure about one token, 49 low-probability tokens stay in play.
  Artificially injecting randomness the model doesn't want.

Top-p adapts:
  Model confident (one token dominates) → nucleus shrinks to 1-2 tokens
  Model uncertain → nucleus expands to include more options
  Respects the model's own confidence. Doesn't override it.

THE MOST IMPORTANT PRODUCTION PATTERN: TWO-STAGE TEMPERATURE
--------------------------------------------------------------
An agent does two fundamentally different things:
  1. DECIDES what action to take  → tool selection, argument generation
  2. GENERATES natural language   → explains results to users

These need completely different temperature settings.

STAGE 1 — Tool selection: temperature=0, top_p=0.1
  Why: correct tool call is not creative. There's a right answer.
  Randomness here = hallucinated tool names, wrong arguments, broken JSON.
  At temp=1: 3% of calls generate "web_Search" instead of "web_search" → crash.

STAGE 2 — Summary generation: temperature=0.3-0.7 (domain-dependent)
  Why: natural language needs variation to not sound robotic.
  temp=0 here = same sentence structure every time = users notice.

TEMPERATURE BY DOMAIN:
  Legal/Medical/Finance: 0.2  (rigid terminology, low variation tolerated)
  General:               0.4  (natural prose)
  Email/Creative:        0.6  (variation is a feature)

FULL IMPLEMENTATION
--------------------
import anthropic
client = anthropic.Anthropic()

def select_tool(messages: list, tools: list):
    """Deterministic tool selection. Never random."""
    return client.messages.create(
        model="claude-sonnet-4-6",
        max_tokens=512,    # tool calls are short JSON blobs
        temperature=0,     # deterministic
        top_p=0.1,         # belt-and-suspenders safety net
        tools=tools,
        messages=messages,
    )

def generate_summary(messages: list, domain: str = "general") -> str:
    """Natural language generation. Domain-calibrated temperature."""
    temperature_by_domain = {
        "legal":   0.2,
        "finance": 0.2,
        "medical": 0.2,
        "general": 0.4,
        "email":   0.6,
    }
    return client.messages.create(
        model="claude-sonnet-4-6",
        max_tokens=2048,
        temperature=temperature_by_domain.get(domain, 0.4),
        top_p=0.9,    # wide nucleus OK for natural language
        messages=messages + [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize findings."}],
    ).content[0].text

def run_agent(user_query: str, tools: list, domain: str = "general") -> str:
    messages = [{"role": "user", "content": user_query}]

    while True:
        response = select_tool(messages, tools)  # temp=0
        messages.append({"role": "assistant", "content": response.content})

        if response.stop_reason == "end_turn":
            break

        if response.stop_reason == "tool_use":
            for block in response.content:
                if block.type == "tool_use":
                    result = execute_tool(block.name, block.input)
                    messages.append({
                        "role": "user",
                        "content": [{"type": "tool_result",
                                     "tool_use_id": block.id,
                                     "content": str(result)}]
                    })

    return generate_summary(messages, domain=domain)  # temp=0.2-0.6

FAILURE MODES
--------------
1. temp=1 ON TOOL CALLS
   3% of calls: "web_Search", "websearch", "search_web" → string match fails → crash
   300 failures/day at 10,000 daily requests. Silent in production.

2. temp=0 ON CREATIVE TASKS
   Cold emails all have identical sentence structure → recipients notice → reply rates drop

3. top_p TOO HIGH FOR STRUCTURED OUTPUT
   Meaningful probability on tokens that break JSON → parse failures at scale

4. PROVIDER TEMPERATURE DIVERGENCE
   temp=0.7 on GPT-4 ≠ temp=0.7 on Claude.
   Different training → different distributions at same temperature.
   Portkey routing across providers needs per-provider calibration.

